<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Zenit PDF — the standard sandbox entitlement set, minus the unused app group and
  minus every network entitlement.

  No com.apple.security.network.client and no network.server: the application makes no network
  connection, so the App Sandbox refuses any socket it might try to open. The update system that
  once needed the client entitlement was removed; a link a person clicks (a markup's web address)
  opens in their browser through Launch Services, which needs no network entitlement here. PDF
  JavaScript never runs, launch actions are ignored, and no telemetry is collected.
  Scripts/verify-no-network.sh fails the build if a network or temporary-exception entitlement
  is attached to any piece of the signed bundle.

  No com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: it existed only because an ad-hoc app
  could not load an ad-hoc embedded framework under Library Validation. The
  bundle embeds no framework now, every dynamic dependency resolves to the operating system, and
  Library Validation stays on. Scripts/verify-bundle-linkage.sh fails the build if an embedded
  library ever appears that Library Validation would refuse.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  No com.apple.security.application-groups (removed 2026-09-23). Nothing used the
	  group container, and on macOS 15 and later an app-group identifier that no
	  provisioning profile authorises — which ad-hoc signing cannot have — makes the system ask
	  the person on first access. A future design that needs shared storage between processes
	  (for example a local automation socket) must not assume a group container exists.
	-->
	<!--
	  File ▸ Print. Without this key the sandbox refuses the print connection and macOS
	  answers ⌘P with its own alert — "This application does not support printing. Please
	  contact the application's developer." — even though printDocument(_:) is fully
	  implemented (LT-23).
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.print</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
